# Vibe Mechanics Codebase Review — Focus: Optimizations

This document presents a comprehensive, structured code audit of the **Vibe Mechanics** codebase located at `C:\dev\vibe-mechanic`. The analysis focuses specifically on rendering efficiency, state management, memory leaks, payload sizes, caching opportunities, API rate limiting, and build configurations.

---

## Executive Summary

The Vibe Mechanics application is a React-based Progressive Web App (PWA) with a Capacitor shell and an Express backend. It integrates the Google Gemini API (via the `@google/genai` SDK) to provide automotive diagnostics, OBD-II code lookups, fluid specifications, maintenance calculators, and visual part scanner features.

While the core functionality is robustly built, this review identifies several critical optimization issues:
1. **Redundant AI Calls**: Switching tabs causes component remounts, triggering redundant network fetches to the Gemini API for static data.
2. **Keystroke Render Storms**: Heavy components render from scratch on every character keystroke because input bindings trigger global state updates.
3. **Huge Image Upload Payloads**: Mobile users uploading photos to the vision endpoint transmit uncompressed base64 images (5-15MB), risking timeouts and mobile performance degradation.
4. **Denial of Service (DoS) Vulnerabilities**: The backend parses body payloads up to 15MB globally across all endpoints instead of isolating it only to the vision endpoint.
5. **No Static Asset Compression / Cache Headers**: The Express server serves production assets without gzip/Brotli compression or Cache-Control headers.

