## 5. API Limits, Caching & Gemini Efficiency

### A. Lack of Server-Side Cache for Semi-Static Automotive Queries
*   **File Reference:**
    *   [server.ts:L343](file:///C:/dev/vibe-mechanic/server.ts#L343) (`/api/obd2` diagnostic route)
    *   [server.ts:L490](file:///C:/dev/vibe-mechanic/server.ts#L490) (`/api/specs` specifications route)
*   **Issues:**
    *   OBD-II codes (e.g. `P0171`) and technical specifications (lug nut torque, spark plug gap) are largely static. Currently, looking up `P0171` for a `2015 Toyota Corolla` makes a live Gemini API request every single time.
    *   This is highly inefficient, costly in tokens, slow (causing 2-5 seconds of latency), and rapidly triggers rate-limiting thresholds.
*   **Recommended Fix:**
    *   Implement a server-side cache (using an in-memory `lru-cache` package or a lightweight SQLite database) to store and immediately return identical query payloads.
    ```typescript
    import { LRUCache } from 'lru-cache';
    const specCache = new LRUCache<string, any>({ max: 1000, ttl: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24 }); // 24hr cache
    ```

### B. Missing Context Caching for Verbose Prompts
*   **File Reference:**
    *   [server.ts:L209](file:///C:/dev/vibe-mechanic/server.ts#L209) (diagnose prompt), [L273](file:///C:/dev/vibe-mechanic/server.ts#L273) (follow-up prompt), [L344](file:///C:/dev/vibe-mechanic/server.ts#L344) (obd2 prompt)
*   **Issues:**
    *   Prompts contain verbose formatting requirements and expert guidelines. Sending these on every request consumes excessive input tokens.
*   **Recommended Fix:**
    *   Store static mechanical constraints as System Instructions. If prompts exceed 32k tokens in future features, activate Gemini API Context Caching to reuse the context block, decreasing processing costs and response latency.

